em all when i need them but  potentially i can get to where i can  engage in meaningful conversations  that's my goal so therefore what i can  say  after three months after two weeks is  not so important to me  second of all i do get criticized you  know why do you spend you know x amount  of time on arabic and then next thing  you're doing persian and then you're  doing  turkish uh and then you can't speak  persian when you're suddenly challenged  to say something in persian  but this is another thing my sort of  input-based learning  is  basically creating the potential to be  able to speak meaningfully  everything that i have done in arabic  and persian is with me i haven't lost  that not only do i have it somewhere in  my brain but furthermore  i have it in all of my content at link  in all of my many stories which i can go  back to it that  almost represents sort of like an  external  hard drive  where i park things  that are part of my learning process  that i can go back to very quickly  refresh and improve on it's part of all  of the sort of  potential in the language that i have  invested in you have to invest up front  in the ability to speak well in the long  run now  eventually  to speak well you have to speak a lot so  if my chinese friend goes to language  class and never speaks doesn't even take  advantage of the opportunity of speaking  with me he's not going to improve to  speak well you have to speak a lot  eventually  if you are worried about how you sound  if you are worried about your mistakes  you won't speak a lot you have to work  your way through  that period where you are making  mistakes and you are doubting yourself  and you just have to go with it all  right but obviously you want to build up  sort of a sort of that that background  in the language so that you have a  chance of understanding  what people are saying and yet you have  a chance of finding words and phrases so  to summarize to speak well you have to  speak a lot my chinese friend my only  advice to him is you probably have  enough words to speak so start speaking  at every opportunity  second of all  what you can say after 10 days or two  weeks or three months is not as  important as what you will be able to  say  in the long run so  i am convinced that in my  three languages that i'm exploring right  now  arabic turkish and um  persian that are all connected  historically culturally that i'm going  to be involved with for  three years perhaps  i have the goal of being able to  read and listen and engage people in  conversations on a variety of subjects  in these three languages as my ultimate  goal so i don't really care that much if  in some random encounter in a restaurant  i struggle to say something so  short-term versus long-term  the  sort of fundamental activity in order to  get that language in you is still  listening and reading however comes a  point where if you want to improve your  speaking you're going to have to speak a  lot you're going to have accept the fact  that sometimes you'll be  tongue-tied and at other times  you'll be able to say more and you just  got to fight your way through it and  keep talking i hope that makes sense by  the way i still not sure where i'm  supposed to look in this my iphone to  make sure that i show up in the middle  of the screen we'll see what happens on  this one  uh what else was i going to say oh yeah  one other thing  i will have my first turkish  conversation tomorrow with a tutor here  at link  one of the content items that i was  using  at link is some tutor reports  with sort of reinhardt one of our link  members speaking to his turkey tutor  and they talk about a variety of things  that relate to reinhardt's experience as  a teacher in austria  i found these very good because it's  interesting to eavesdrop on reinhardt  and his conversation with his tutor  the  content consists of  full sentences and full phrases  the voice of the turkish  narrator is extremely pleasant to listen  to  i find this very useful learning content  for beginners  and i'd be interested in reactions from  people if i were to and in fact i can  put up my uh tutor conversations with my  persian tutors because we don't have a  lot of content for persian i wonder if  people would find these interesting i  would like to hear more of this kind of  material for turkish because i'm still  at a very early stage in my turkish and  this kind of you know variety  is great so that the the mini stories  are great because there's a lot of  repetition but  having a chance to peek into reinhardt's  conversation with his tutor is  interesting to me it introduces variety  and he  the the words and phrases that she reads  are the  words and phrases that i want to be able  to use in my conversation tomorrow with  my tutor  look forward to react to your reactions  bye for now  you
 

